1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:02,530
Welcome! In this tutorial we're going to write

2
00:00:02,530 --> 00:00:05,250
our very first Python program, and don't worry

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:06,990
if you've never written a single line of

4
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:09,790
code before. We'll start from the very beginning.

5
00:00:09,790 --> 00:00:12,530
Our goal is simple. Make Python display the

6
00:00:12,530 --> 00:00:16,010
message "Hello World on the Screen". First, open

7
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:18,880
your favorite code editor. We'll use Visual Studio

8
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:21,950
Code, but any Python editor will work. Create

9
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:24,970
a new file and name it "hello.py". The

10
00:00:24,970 --> 00:00:27,120
.py extension tells us that this is a

11
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,040
Python source file. Now, inside the file, type

12
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:33,040
the following line of code. Print "Hello World".

13
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:35,790
That's it. You've just written your first Python

14
00:00:35,790 --> 00:00:38,480
program. But before we run it, let's understand

15
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,770
what's happening here. The word "print" is a

16
00:00:40,770 --> 00:00:43,760
built-in Python function. Its job is to display

17
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,770
information in the output. Next, we have parentheses.

18
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:49,120
These allow us to pass information into the

19
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:52,620
function. Inside the parentheses, we've written "Hello World"

20
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:56,420
surrounded by quotation marks. In Python, text enclosed

21
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:58,830
in quotation marks is called a string. The

22
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:01,560
quotation marks tell Python to treat "Hello World"

23
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:04,160
as text, rather than a variable or instruction.

24
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:07,360
Now, let's run our program. Save the file,

25
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:11,790
open your terminal and type "PythonHello.py". On some

26
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:16,200
systems, you'll need to use Python3Hello.py instead. Press

27
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,130
enter. And there it is, "Hello World", Python

28
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:21,920
executed our code, and printed the message directly

29
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,280
into the terminal. Let's try changing something. Replace

30
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,420
"Hello World" with "print". I'm learning Python. Run

31
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:30,990
the program again. Notice how the output changes

32
00:01:30,990 --> 00:01:34,960
immediately. Now, here's something interesting. The print function

33
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,110
isn't limited to displaying text. We can also

34
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:40,380
use it to display numbers and perform calculations.

35
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:45,100
For example, type "print5+3". Run the program. Python

36
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:48,120
displays 8. Notice that we didn't use quotation

37
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:50,730
marks this time. That's because Python evaluates the

38
00:01:50,730 --> 00:01:53,840
mathematical expression, instead of treating it as text.

39
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,420
And that's the basic idea behind programming. We

40
00:01:56,420 --> 00:02:00,890
write instructions and the computer executes them. Congratulations.

41
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:04,220
You've written, executed, and modified your first Python

42
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:07,600
program. In the next tutorial, we'll explore variables

43
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,510
and learn how Python stores information. See you

44
00:02:10,510 --> 00:02:11,120
there.

